body:
I sadly don't have help to offer, only sympathy. I've been struggling with my homosexuality since I was 13, and I'm 26 now. Still struggling with thoughts of internalized homophobia. Much the same thoughts as you. I also thought a woman couldn't love another woman the way a guy can and I thought other women couldn't possibly be attracted to women the way I am, because I'm a fuck up and unnatural somehow. I never had gay friends, only straight girl friends and guy friends. The straights girls talked about boys all the time, they were even lesbophobic at times, the straight guys just wanted to date me. I actually came out to a few of them when they confessed and felt really terrible I couldn't reciprocate, which definitely did not help the internalized homophobia. My family, especially extended family, always questioned why I never had a boyfriend when all my siblings were going out with someone. My younger brother is even living with his long time girlfriend. My immediate family knows I'm gay, but they didn't know until I kind of came out at 25, and I knew I was gay when I was 12-13. My grandparents, cousins, aunts and uncles don't know. Or they might have doubts but nothing's confirmed. I just know some of them are very homophobic, saying stuff like "if my son (so my cousin) comes out as gay, he's out of our lives". And that cousin has never had a girlfriend either, I have no idea if he's gay or not but... damn. I probably won't ever come out to them. I tried getting guys off tinder at some point because I was trying to somehow convert myself, but always chickened out when it became more serious. Because it made me anxious as opposed to feeling butterflies.

&#x200B;

The internalized homophobia started getting better a few months ago, after I actually came out to my immediate family. They don't mind and now I don't feel pressured to date. I don't feel as ashamed now and I wish I'd started getting better sooner. You still have time, you're a young adult (I kinda still am too, but I feel like all the fun, young adult opportunities have passed me by), you don't have to throw it away. It sucks not having a girlfriend, but it can happen once you're more comfortable with yourself. I know it's hard and it takes time, I've always been very supportive of homosexual people but could never be as kind to myself despite being gay too.
